The theory you actually need

Logo replication is 90% about rebuilding with the right construction method, not freehand drawing. Four concepts to learn deliberately:

Boolean operations (Union, Subtract, Intersect, Exclude) — how negative-space logos (FedEx arrow, WWF panda) are actually built from overlapping shapes, not drawn by hand.
The arc/ellipse tool + stroke properties — for anything like Spotify's bars: draw a full circle, apply a stroke, then use the arc/angle property to cut it down to a segment. This is how you get mathematically perfect, evenly-spaced arcs instead of hand-drawn curves.
Grids & optical vs. mathematical alignment — most corporate logos were built on strict geometric grids (circles, squares dividing into halves/thirds). Recreating the "logo grid" breakdown (search "[brand] logo construction grid") before building teaches you why proportions feel right.
Pen tool + bezier handles — for swoosh/flowing logos (Nike, Amazon arrow). This is the actual hard skill — learn to place minimum anchor points with symmetric handles rather than many messy points.

